This raw-file policy is bypassable on the legacy same-origin `/preview/files/` route, so it does not fully deliver "page script cannot reach the loopback control API" for raw `.html`.

`connect-src 'none'` only constrains fetch/XHR issued from *this document's own realm*. It has no `frame-src`/`child-src`/`object-src`/`worker-src`, and there is no `default-src` to backstop them. On the legacy route the preview is same-origin with the API (`http://127.0.0.1:<port>`), and the daemon sets no global `X-Frame-Options`/`frame-ancestors` (nothing in `internal/httpd` sets them). So a malicious `index.html` served under this policy (scripts run) can reach the sink through a nested same-origin iframe:

```js
const f = document.createElement('iframe');
f.src = '/api/v1/health';        // any same-origin API doc; its response carries NO CSP
f.onload = () => f.contentWindow.fetch('/api/v1/projects/ID/config', {
  method: 'PUT',
  headers: { 'Content-Type': 'application/json' },
  body: '{"postCreate":"..."}',
});
document.body.appendChild(f);
```

The `fetch` executes in the child realm, whose response has no CSP, so the parent's `connect-src 'none'` does not apply. (An `about:blank` iframe inherits the parent policy and would be blocked, but a *navigated* same-origin document gets its own empty policy, which is the hole.) The Markdown policy is unaffected because `default-src 'none'` implies `frame-src 'none'`.

Suggested fix: add `frame-src 'none'; child-src 'none'; object-src 'none'; worker-src 'none'` to `previewFileCSP` (or use `default-src 'none'` and re-allow only `script-src`/`style-src`/`img-src`/`font-src`). Note this may restrict a built app that legitimately uses iframes/workers, so it is a real trade-off worth calling out.

Two gaps here, both one-liners on the existing test:

1. No case for `kind == ""` (unset). That's the path every existing `ao spawn` takes, since `--kind` defaults to empty, and it now flows through the new branch. Worth pinning that it still resolves to `worker.agent`.
2. The new error branch at spawn.go:323 is untested. An orchestrator spawn against a project with no `orchestrator.agent` is the behavior this PR actually changes, and nothing asserts it returns the `--orchestrator-agent` message.

```go
if got, err := resolveSpawnHarness("", "", project); err != nil || got != "codex" {
	t.Fatalf("unset kind: got %q err %v, want codex", got, err)
}

noOrch := projectDetails{ID: "demo", Config: &projectConfig{Worker: roleOverride{Agent: "codex"}}}
if _, err := resolveSpawnHarness("", "orchestrator", noOrch); err == nil ||
	!strings.Contains(err.Error(), "--orchestrator-agent") {
	t.Fatalf("orchestrator without orchestrator.agent: got err %v, want --orchestrator-agent hint", err)
}
```

The second one matters most, since it's the only genuinely new failure path in the change.

`stop` has `TestReviewStopTooManyArgsIsUsageError` but `restart` has no equivalent, which looks accidental rather than deliberate. Both commands use the same `atMostOneArg`, so the coverage should be symmetric:

```go
func TestReviewRestartTooManyArgsIsUsageError(t *testing.T) {
	setConfigEnv(t)
	_, _, err := executeCLI(t, aliveDeps(), "review", "restart", "mer-1", "mer-2")
	if got := ExitCode(err); got != 2 {
		t.Fatalf("exit code = %d, want 2 (usage); err=%v", got, err)
	}
}
```

This is the assertion that hides the bug above: `key: "`"` together with `shift: true` isn't a state Electron can deliver on a US layout, where Shift+backtick yields `~`.

Once the matcher keys off `code`, the regression test worth having is the one written in terms of what the runtime actually sends:

```ts
it("matches Ctrl+Shift+` even though the character arrives shifted", () => {
	expect(matchesNewShellTerminalShortcut(chord({ code: "Backquote", key: "~", ctrl: true, shift: true }), false)).toBe(true);
});
```

That one fails against the current implementation, which is exactly what you want from it.

This path has no test, and there's an established place for one right next door.

`XtermTerminal.test.tsx` already has roughly 25 key-handler tests driving exactly this mechanism through `attachCustomKeyEventHandler`, including close neighbours like "leaves plain Ctrl+C as terminal input on non-Windows even when text is selected" and "does not re-fire a shortcut on the keyup that follows its keydown". So the harness is already there:

```ts
it("sends meta-return for Shift+Enter and a plain CR for Enter", () => {
	// Shift+Enter -> ESC+CR, consumed rather than passed to xterm
	// Enter -> falls through to xterm's default CR
});
```

The keyup half is worth covering too, given the existing test at line 445 exists precisely because that bug has bitten this handler before — the early `event.type` return should already cover it, but that's the kind of thing worth pinning.

As it stands the well-tested half of this PR is the dialog, which is also the half that isn't platform- or TUI-sensitive.

This doc comment is good and does the job it claims. The other half of the pair doesn't exist though.

The PR description says "a doc comment on `appendModelFlag` **and an inline comment on `GetRestoreCommand`** both point back to this decision." `GetRestoreCommand` is untouched by this diff and carries no such comment.

That matters a little more than a typical missing comment, because the scenario you're defending against is specifically someone opening `GetRestoreCommand` to "just mirror Codex" — and that person is looking at the function you didn't annotate. They'd have to already know to check `appendModelFlag`, or discover it by tripping the test.

`TestGetRestoreCommandDoesNotForwardModelOverride` does catch it, so the safeguard genuinely holds and this isn't a correctness issue. But the breadcrumb you designed for is the cheaper of the two to hit, and it's a two-liner:

```go
func (p *Plugin) GetRestoreCommand(ctx context.Context, cfg ports.RestoreConfig) (cmd []string, ok bool, err error) {
	// Deliberately does not forward cfg.Config.Model: --model + --session
	// composition is unverified against a real kimi install (see #2890 and
	// appendModelFlag). Pinned by TestGetRestoreCommandDoesNotForwardModelOverride.
```

This is the strip @Vaibhaav-Tiwari flagged, and the width really doesn't shrink on collapse.

`--ao-sidebar-w` is set on `:root` by `useResizable` (Sidebar.tsx:187) and tracks only the **resize-drag** width. Collapsing never writes to it — the collapsed rail uses a separate token, `--size-sidebar-icon: 48px`, wired through `--sidebar-width-icon` on the provider. So this stays at the expanded width regardless of collapse state.

Working the numbers out of `tokens.css`:

| | value |
|---|---|
| strip width | `--ao-sidebar-w`, default **240px**, up to **420px** after a resize |
| collapsed sidebar | `--size-sidebar-icon`, **48px** |
| strip height | `--size-traffic-light-clearance`, **36px** |
| panel surface top edge | **14px** (`--size-center-panel-inset`; `<main>` carries no macOS top pad) |

So when collapsed there's an invisible `WebkitAppRegion: drag` region of roughly **192 × 22px** sitting over the top-left of the center panel surface — and up to **372px wide** for anyone who has widened their sidebar. On macOS that's exactly where this PR relocates the crumb and the New task / Orchestrator / bell controls, so the top slice of that row swallows clicks into a window drag.

The fix is slightly more involved than swapping the class, because this JSX lives in the component that *renders* `SidebarProvider` — so it's outside the provider's React context and can't call `useSidebar()` here. It needs to move into a child:

```tsx
function MacWindowDragStrip() {
	const { state } = useSidebar();
	return (
		<div
			aria-hidden="true"
			className={cn(
				"fixed top-0 left-0 z-chrome h-traffic-light-clearance",
				state === "collapsed"
					? "w-(--size-sidebar-icon)"
					: "w-(--ao-sidebar-w,var(--size-sidebar-default))",
			)}
			style={{ WebkitAppRegion: "drag" } as CSSProperties}
		/>
	);
}
```

then render `<MacWindowDragStrip />` in place of this block. `useSidebar` is already exported from `ui/sidebar.tsx` and exposes `state: "expanded" | "collapsed"`, so nothing else has to change.

Worth a small unit test asserting the width class flips with collapse state — this is invisible by construction, so a regression here would only ever be found by someone reporting that clicks mysteriously don't work.

Good catch, fixed in a29694025. spawnEnv now uses:

    if runFilePath != "" {
        env[EnvRunFile] = runFilePath
    } else {
        delete(env, EnvRunFile)
    }

so an empty daemon path drops any project-supplied AO_RUN_FILE, restoring both invariants (empty path leaves it unset; a project cannot override the AO-owned var). Added the combined regression case to TestSpawnEnvRunFile asserting AO_RUN_FILE is absent for empty path + project override; verified it fails without the delete branch.

**[High] Capture-phase `stopPropagation()` silently kills every anchor's own click handler**

`document.addEventListener("click", handler, true)` fires before React's root-container listeners, so `stopPropagation()` here means no anchor's React `onClick` ever sees the event.

I verified this rather than pattern-matching it. Temporary probe added to the shell suite:

```tsx
it("PROBE: does an anchor's own click handler still run?", async () => {
  vi.spyOn(apiClient, "POST").mockResolvedValue({} as never);
  await renderShell();
  const card = document.body.appendChild(document.createElement("div"));
  card.dataset.sessionId = "sess-1";
  const anchor = card.appendChild(Object.assign(document.createElement("a"), { href: "https://github.com/o/r/pull/1" }));
  const own = vi.fn();
  anchor.addEventListener("click", own);
  fireEvent.click(anchor);
  expect(own).toHaveBeenCalled();
});
```

Result: `AssertionError: expected "vi.fn()" to be called at least once`.

Concrete regression: `NotificationCenter.tsx:369-374`. That PR-notification anchor's `onClick` runs `event.preventDefault(); onOpenPrimary(notification)`, and `openPrimary` (`NotificationCenter.tsx:65-75`) fires `captureRendererEvent("ao.renderer.notification_opened")` plus `window.open(prUrl)`, with `openAndDismiss` (line 148) closing the panel. The notification bell lives in the topbar, so on any session route a session is in scope and this handler wins: no telemetry event, the panel stays open, and the PR loads into the session Browser panel instead of the OS browser. No existing test catches it, because `NotificationCenter` tests render the component without the shell.

Suggested fix: drop `stopPropagation()` (`preventDefault()` alone already suppresses navigation) and bail early on `if (event.defaultPrevented) return;`, or register on the bubble phase so element handlers run first.

**[Medium] `new URL(anchor.href)` is unguarded and runs before the cheap checks**

`closest<HTMLAnchorElement>("a[href]")` also matches SVG `<a>` elements, whose `href` is an `SVGAnimatedString`, not a string. `new URL()` then throws inside a document-level capture listener. The `TerminalPane.tsx:290-295` equivalent wraps the identical call in `try`/`catch` for exactly this reason.

Two changes: move the `event.button !== 0` test above this line (it is the cheapest bail), and wrap the parse:

```ts
let url: URL;
try {
  url = new URL(anchor.href);
} catch {
  return;
}
```

**[Medium] Duplicated link policy instead of the shared helper that was requested**

@illegalcall asked for "a shared app-level link policy." This effect reimplements `TerminalPane.handleLinkOpen` (`TerminalPane.tsx:287-318`): same URL/protocol parsing, same `setInspectorView` + `setInspectorOpen`, same preview POST, same `invalidateQueries`. Two copies is how the guard and error-handling gaps flagged above appeared in the first place, and how the next ones will.

