**[P2] The Windows PowerShell fallback still cannot invoke the newly quoted runtime.** When Git Bash is absent, MiniMax Code 0.6.5's actual compatible-hook planner selects `powershell.exe -NoProfile -NonInteractive -Command` and forwards the expanded command unchanged. `writeHookConfig` now emits:

```powershell
"C:\Users\Review User\.minimax-code\runtime\node.exe" "C:\Users\Review User\.ao\agents\minimax-code\review\plugins\ao-activity/hook.cjs" bGF1bmNo
```

PowerShell treats the leading quoted path as a string expression; it needs the call operator `&` to invoke that executable. This is specified in [Microsoft's call-operator documentation](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/powershell/module/microsoft.powershell.core/about/about_operators#call-operator-). The native runner adds no call operator before spawning the returned plan. Consequently the hooks, including the standing-context hook, cannot run on this fallback. MiniMax's compatible-hook schema accepts `command` plus `args`, and its planner uses those directly with `shell: false`. Emitting the raw Node path as `command` and the hook path / launch ID as separate `args` avoids this shell-specific quoting problem; the direct-argv control below verifies that native plan. AO requires a usable Git executable, but does not guarantee Git Bash.

