```

Observed HTTP 200, `observation.ok:true`, `observation.state.screenshot_file_path` pointing to that caller-selected file, and `images:[]`. The real driver newly wrote a valid **31,708-byte PNG** outside AO's computer-use image folder, despite the capture opt-out; the image was not attached to the tool result.

Sanitize read-step output paths in both step forms before the driver's trailing-read merge, then add only AO-owned paths when capture is allowed. [Pinned merge behavior](https://github.com/trycua/cua/blob/2d2f99228d4d2508dc6fcc84c176b296e146714a/libs/cua-driver/rust/crates/cua-driver-core/src/batch_tools.rs#L472-L518).

Fixed in 25952d3fd1 with your second option. The desktop remembers each shared file's link id in localStorage (which lives under `~/.ao/electron`), keyed by control plane, user, session id and path. Sharing that file again sends `PUT /me/artifact-shares/{id}`, so renaming the session keeps the same link and slot, and the link serves the new version. If the remembered link is gone (JSON 404, revoked or deleted) or names another file (`409 artifact_share_name_changed`), the desktop forgets it and falls back to `POST` by project, feature and name, then remembers the new id. A plain-text 404 is still "sharing isn't available on this server".

I didn't send the session id as `feature`, because local ids are per-project counters on each machine (`agent-orchestrator-1`). The same user sharing `report.html` from `agent-orchestrator-1` on two laptops would then replace each other's link. With the remembered id, each machine keeps its own link and a rename doesn't use a slot. The one gap: if localStorage is cleared, or the file is shared from another machine, the first share publishes by name again.

Tests in `artifact-share.test.ts`:
- a second share after a rename goes to `PUT .../{id}?name=` and returns the same link;
- a stale link (404 or name changed) falls back to POST and remembers the new id;
- links stay separate per user, session and file;
- a plain-text 404 on the PUT still reports the server as unavailable.

**1. Blocking: workers started before this deploy can no longer push.**

The worker only self-updates on start, so a sandbox that is already running keeps its old credential helper and PR bridge after the CP deploy:
- the old helper asks `/worker/github-token` for a push token, which now returns `contents:read`, so GitHub answers 403;
- the old PR bridge calls `/worker/push-grant`, which is removed, so it gets 404.

Reproduced on staging: session `523e458f` was started on the previous release, pushed fine, then after the deploy:

```
$ git push -u origin ao-review-7201-old2-1793036794
remote: Permission to Pritom14/Assignment11.3.git denied to aoagentsdev123[bot].
fatal: unable to access 'https://github.com/Pritom14/Assignment11.3.git/': The requested URL returned error: 403
```

Prod runs keep-warm, so about 55 live sessions would lose push and PR creation until each worker restarts.

Example fix, a one-release compatibility path. The old helper always sends `?repo=owner/name` (new workers never call this endpoint with it), so that can select the legacy behaviour:

```go
// Compatibility for workers started before the git proxy (their helper asks
// here for a push token with ?repo=owner/name). Remove once every pre-proxy
// worker has restarted, e.g. one release after this ships.
if repo := workerRequestedRepository(r); repo != "" {
	grant, err := s.checkoutBroker.IssueLegacyPushGrantForRepo(r.Context(), claims.OrgID, claims.SessionID, repo)
	if err == nil {
		s.logger.Info("served a legacy push token to a pre-proxy worker", "session_id", claims.SessionID)
		writeJSON(w, http.StatusOK, worker.GitHubTokenResponse{Token: grant.Token, ExpiresAt: grant.ExpiresAt})
		return
	}
}
```

(`workerRequestedRepository` and the legacy push grant are what this PR removes; they would come back only for the window), plus keeping `POST /worker/push-grant` for the same window. Use a freshly minted per-repo write token there (the old `IssuePushGrantForRepo`), not the proxy's cached one, so the proxy's tokens still never leave the CP. If the worker reports its build anywhere (heartbeat, bootstrap), gating on "pre-proxy build" is tighter than gating on `?repo=`. The other option is to restart every worker right after the deploy, but that interrupts running agents.

**5. Follow-up: projects that reach GitHub only through a PAT can no longer clone.**

`checkout-grant` and the git proxy only use the App now, so a session whose repository has no App grant gets `403 CHECKOUT_NOT_AUTHORIZED` (as `TestWorkerCheckoutGrantNeverHandsOutAPAT` expects). The description says no PAT is handed to a sandbox, which is right, but it does not mention that PAT-only checkouts stop working. Prod impact is small (2 of 282 GitHub sessions in the last 30 days had no App grant, one org, none live), but it is a silent behaviour change.

Example fix that keeps the PAT out of the sandbox: use it server-side in the proxy as the App's fallback, the same way raise and claim already do:

```go
// gitProxyToken, after the App grant fails with ErrForbidden or ErrNotFound:
if pat, ok := s.workerGitHubPATGrant(ctx, claims); ok && sameGitHubRepository(pat.CloneURL, fullName) {
	return pat.Token, nil // attached to the upstream request only, never returned
}
```

and let `workerCheckoutGrant` return `{cloneUrl}` from the PAT's clone URL in that case. Or, if dropping PAT checkouts is intended, say so in the description and the support runbook.

Deny entries only apply when the denied text is the command base, so chained or redirected commands headed by an allowed one still get through: `git status && git push origin HEAD` has base `git status` and never reaches `Shell(git push)`, and `git diff --output=/path/to/file` (also `git log --output=...`, `git show --output=...`) writes an arbitrary file while matching `Shell(git diff)`. That is the same mechanism as the `printf x > file` case documented here, just moved from `printf` to the entries that remain.

Cursor's matcher cannot express "no shell operators", so rules alone cannot close this — closing it needs a real sandbox. At minimum, the comment should not promise more than the rules deliver, since the PR body's "can't push or write files" reads as a hard guarantee:

```suggestion
// reviewerAllowedPermissions names subcommands, not whole tools: a bare
// Shell(git) admitted `git push` without a prompt, and Shell(printf) admitted
// `printf x > file` (both verified live). Anything else prompts, so an
// unattended Cursor reviewer stops instead of acting. Cursor matches rules on
// the command base, so a chained command with an allowed head
// (`git status && git push`) and a write through an allowed head
// (`git diff --output=<file>`) are still admitted: this profile keeps the
// reviewer on the read path, it is not a hard boundary.
```

This pins the literal rules but not the property that matters: that the profile can still run the commands the reviewer is told to run. As written it passes with an allow list that makes review submission impossible (see the comment on `Shell(ao review submit)`). `claudecode` has the useful version of this check — `TestAllowlistCoversPromptRequiredPipedCommands` asserts the allow list covers each prompt-required command, including the `printf |` prefix.

```suggestion
	for _, rule := range reviewerAllowedPermissions {
		switch rule {
		case "Shell(git)", "Shell(gh)", "Shell(ao)":
			t.Fatalf("allowlist admits a whole tool: %q", rule)
		}
	}
	// The review prompt pipes its JSON through printf, so the profile must keep
	// printf and the gh api submit call allowed or every review stalls at the
	// first approval prompt.
	for _, want := range []string{"Shell(printf)", "Shell(gh api)", "Shell(ao review submit)"} {
		found := false
		for _, rule := range reviewerAllowedPermissions {
			found = found || rule == want
		}
		if !found {
			t.Fatalf("allowlist missing prompt-required %q: %#v", want, reviewerAllowedPermissions)
		}
	}
```

Note the `Shell(printf)` case is dropped from the whole-tool check above, so the two assertions stay consistent with the allow list once `printf` is restored. Also, comparing `env[cursorConfigDirEnv]` to `env[cursorDataDirEnv]` only shows the two agree with each other, not that they point at the directory `installReviewerConfig` writes; that holds today because both go through `reviewerProfileDir`, but the assertion would not catch a divergence.
